// The struct CALL form takes a record VALUE, not only a record literal.
//
// `MyApp({context: c, port: 8080})` always worked. `MyApp(defaults())` — the
// same coercion with the field set arriving as a value — was a type error:
// `checkStructCallForm` read the argument SYNTACTICALLY and reported
// "MyApp expects an anonymous struct literal {...} matching its fields, got
// {port: Int, context: Context}", naming as unacceptable a type that is
// exactly MyApp's own field set.
//
// The restriction was the CHECKER's alone. The evaluator's constructor takes
// any struct value, copies its fields into a NAME-keyed map, fills the
// declaration's defaults and brands the result with the module-qualified
// type name — so the run-time behaviour asserted below was already correct
// under the rejected spelling, and the checker was refusing a program its
// own evaluator ran.
//
// Three properties are asserted together because each is a way the coercion
// could be wrong and only one of them is visible by reading:
//
//   - FIELD ORDER. `Config` declares `{host, port, retries}` and `defaults`
//     returns `{retries, host}` — a different order and a subset. The match
//     is by name, so neither matters. A positional copy would put a String
//     where an Int goes.
//   - DEFAULTS. `retries` is supplied and `port` is not, so `port` comes
//     from the declaration. Omitting a field with no default is an error
//     naming that field, exactly as the literal form reports it.
//   - NOMINAL IDENTITY. The result must be indistinguishable from the same
//     value built with `Config{...}`, or it misses every impl silently:
//     `==` degrades to a structural comparison that answers False on the
//     mismatched name, and `Display` renders the anonymous form.
struct Config {
    host: String
    port: Int = 8080
    retries: Int
}

derive Display for Config

fn defaults(): {retries: Int, host: String} {
    {retries: 3, host: "localhost"}
}

test "the struct call form accepts a record value" {
    built = Config(defaults())
    attached = Config{host: "localhost", port: 8080, retries: 3}

    assert built.host == "localhost"
    assert built.port == 8080
    assert built.retries == 3
    assert built == attached
    assert Display.to_string(built) == "Config{host: localhost, port: 8080, retries: 3}"
}
